SUMMARY:Un-Coding AI in the Classroom: 2-Day Workshop for Faculty and Inst
 ructors
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261211
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261211
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54316749260434
DESCRIPTION:Generative AI is reshaping the landscape of higher education\,
  raising important questions for teaching\, learning\, and academic integr
 ity. Designed for faculty and instructors across all disciplines and requi
 ring no prior technical background\, this two-day workshop unpacks genAI a
 nd offers practical strategies for the university classroom. Whether you a
 re curious about how these models work\, eager to integrate AI into your t
 eaching\, or cautious about its impact on student learning\, this series o
 ffers a supportive\, nuanced space to explore. Participants will gain a cl
 ear foundational understanding of large language models (LLMs)\, evaluate 
 the opportunities and limitations of genAI\, and engage in hands-on practi
 ce configuring customized and privacy-protected AI learning environments f
 or their courses.\n\n \n\nThe workshop runs Thursday-Friday December 10-11
  from 9 AM - 4 PM daily with a break for lunch. Full participation in both
  days is recommended as topics build across sessions.\n\n \n\nDay 1: Un-co
 ding AI\n\nDay 1 strips away the technical jargon to examine what genAI ac
 tually is — and what it isn't. Taking a no-code approach\, we begin by e
 xploring how LLMs process and generate language using intuitive\, non-math
 ematical concepts. We examine how these models are developed and generate 
 responses\, explore the root causes of hallucinations\, trace the influenc
 e of algorithmic bias\, and discuss the impacts of genAI on the environmen
 t\, society and self. \n\n \n\nMoving into practical engagement\, we will 
 discuss pedagogical and prompting strategies that keep human judgment and 
 critical thinking firmly at the center of the learning process while safeg
 uarding privacy. At the end of the day we will review institutional techno
 logy stack options (including the RockyAI implementation in Canvas\, Googl
 e Gemini\, and open-source models) to help you weigh the pros\, cons\, and
  privacy considerations as you build a practical roadmap for considering g
 enAI integration into your courses.\n\n \n\nDay 2: AI in the Classroom\n\n
 Building on Day 1's foundational concepts\, Day 2 transitions into an inte
 ractive\, hands-on workshop focused on implementing privacy-protected AI t
 ools directly into your curriculum. Participants will work step-by-step to
  configure course-ready environments on their own devices. We will explore
  an implementation of RockyAI embedded within Canvas for 24/7 student supp
 ort grounded in instructor-approved course materials\, set up local open-s
 ource models via Ollama to ensure complete data privacy and minimal enviro
 nmental resource impact\, and finally how to leverage the campus-licensed 
 Google Gemini suite. By the end of this session\, you will have functional
  AI tools set up for your courses along with the knowledge and confidence 
 to guide your students in using them effectively to enhance their learning
 .\n\n \n\nBy participating in both days of this workshop\, faculty and ins
 tructors will be able to:\n\nDemystify Core Concepts: Explain the basic pr
 inciples\, capabilities\, and limitations of large language models without
  relying on technical or mathematical jargon.\n\nValidate & Evaluate Outpu
 ts: Apply systematic validation strategies to detect and explore hallucina
 tions\, bias\, and factual inaccuracies in AI-generated content.\n\nArticu
 late Impacts: Describe the environmental\, social\, cognitive and psycholo
 gical impacts of genAI use and common teaching and learning pitfalls.\n\nN
 avigate Campus Tech Stacks: Evaluate the privacy protections\, costs\, and
  practical trade-offs of various AI environments (including Canvas integra
 tions\, enterprise suites\, and local models).\n\nDeploy Privacy-Protected
  Tools: Configure and manage course-specific\, privacy-focused AI assistan
 ts (such as Rocky AI\, Google Gemini\, and Ollama) to support student lear
 ning.\n\nDesign Pedagogy: Develop practical course-based roadmaps and effe
 ctive prompting strategy examples to promote your student’s cognition\, 
 critical thinking\, and academic integrity while protecting personal and i
 nstitutional data.\n \n\nParticipants should bring their laptops* and char
 ger. Bringing a reusable cup or water bottle is encouraged. Coffe\, tea\, 
 and lunch is available for those who register in advance.\n\n*Full partici
 pation in Day 2's activities requires that participants have permissions t
 o install and run software locally on their laptop.\n\n \n\nQuestions? Con
 tact us at datalab@ucdavis.edu.
LOCATION:DataLab Classroom\, Shields Library\, 360
URL:https://events.library.ucdavis.edu/event/ai-for-instructors-2026
X-SOURCE:UC Davis Library
END:VEVENT
